<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
    The schema for Metis, the comprehension check that follows a programming exercise. After the due date each
    student is asked a few questions about their own solution, generated by an LLM from the diff of their repository.
    The student answers inside a per-question time limit and an instructor-defined window, and the LLM rates each
    answer on a Likert scale with a rationale and may ask a follow-up when an answer is weak. Nothing here is graded:
    no result is written. Four new tables, one changeSet each, followed by the check constraints on the bounded
    columns of the configuration and of the questions, and the configuration rows of the programming exercises that
    already exist. No existing table changes.

    metis_exercise_config   one row per programming exercise, a permanent configuration row like the team assignment
                            and plagiarism detection rows every exercise owns. It is inserted with the column defaults
                            when the exercise is stored, whether or not the Metis module is enabled on the server, and
                            the last changeSet gives every programming exercise that already exists its row. Switching
                            Metis on or off only flips the enabled flag, so the settings survive. The row holds the
                            exercise key as its primary key rather than a surrogate id, which expresses the one-to-one
                            in the schema and needs no additional unique index (the shape of user_vcs_access_token).
                            Nothing on the exercise points back, so a configuration cannot outlive its exercise.
    metis_group_window      one row per (exercise, tutorial group) whose answering window differs from the default
                            in the configuration. The schema cannot check that the group belongs to the course of
                            the exercise, since neither table holds that course; the code that writes a window has
                            to check it.
    metis_check             one row per student participation: the state of that student's check, the commit the
                            questions were generated from, and when it was generated, started and completed.
    metis_question          one row per question, main or follow-up, together with the student's answer and the
                            LLM's rating. Question, answer and rating have the same grain, so separate tables would
                            cost a second key and a second lock for nothing. A follow-up is a row that names its
                            main question in parent_question_id, and the key on it covers the check as well, so a
                            follow-up can only point at a question of its own check. The schema does not check that
                            this question is a main question; keeping follow-ups one level deep is up to the code
                            that writes them.

    The per-student data hangs off the participation rather than the user or the exercise, because the
    participation is what every deletion path already reaches. Deleting a participation, deleting an exercise,
    resetting a course or an exam, the data retention cleanup and the deletion of an account all end in deleted
    participation rows, and ON DELETE CASCADE carries that on to the check and its questions without a service
    having to know that Metis exists. No table here references jhi_user, so account deletion has nothing to do
    beyond what it already does.

    Every foreign key cascades on delete, since a row in these tables has no meaning without the row it names, and
    restricts on update, since a primary key is never rewritten.

    An index on a foreign key column is created BEFORE the constraint, so InnoDB adopts it as the key's supporting
    index instead of silently creating a second one of its own, and PostgreSQL, which creates no index for a foreign
    key at all, does not scan the table in full on every cascade. Where a unique key already leads with the column
    (exercise_id on metis_group_window, participation_id on metis_check, check_id on metis_question), no separate
    index is created: it would be a leftmost prefix of the unique key and carry nothing new. The primary key of
    metis_exercise_config covers its own foreign key the same way. The key from a follow-up to its main question
    spans two columns, (parent_question_id, check_id), and gets an index on exactly that pair.

    Enumerations are varchar columns holding the constant name, not native ENUM types: they read the same on both
    databases, a new constant is a code change rather than a migration, and the mapping is a plain EnumType.STRING.
    No column holds JSON. Every value the application reads or filters on has a column of its own, so nothing is
    parsed on the way out and no query has to work around a type that cannot be compared or made distinct.
    -->

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-01-create-metis-exercise-config" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>Create the per-exercise Metis configuration, keyed by the exercise it configures.</comment>
        <createTable tableName="metis_exercise_config">
            <column name="exercise_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" primaryKeyName="pk_metis_exercise_config" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!--
                Off by default. The row exists for every programming exercise from the moment it is stored, inserted
                with nothing but the exercise key, so every value below starts as the column default; switching Metis on
                or off later only changes this flag and keeps the other settings.
            -->
            <column name="enabled" type="boolean" defaultValueBoolean="false">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- The default answering window. A null start means from the due date of the exercise, a null end means no end. -->
            <column name="window_start" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <column name="window_end" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <column name="main_question_count" type="tinyint" defaultValueNumeric="3">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="max_follow_ups_per_question" type="tinyint" defaultValueNumeric="2">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- The LLM picks a time limit per question inside [min, max]. Seconds, so int rather than tinyint. -->
            <column name="min_seconds_per_question" type="int" defaultValueNumeric="60">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="max_seconds_per_question" type="int" defaultValueNumeric="300">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- Both on a scale from 1 (much shorter, much simpler) to 5 (much longer, much more complex); 3 is neutral. -->
            <column name="answer_length" type="tinyint" defaultValueNumeric="3">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="complexity" type="tinyint" defaultValueNumeric="3">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- 5 or 7 points and nothing in between (changeSet 10); every llm_score in metis_question is read against it. -->
            <column name="likert_scale_points" type="tinyint" defaultValueNumeric="5">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- A score at or below the threshold triggers a follow-up question. -->
            <column name="follow_up_threshold" type="tinyint" defaultValueNumeric="3">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- When set, generated questions wait for an instructor's approval before a student sees them. -->
            <column name="review_required" type="boolean" defaultValueBoolean="false">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- Free text handed to the LLM with every generation, e.g. learning goals or files to leave alone. -->
            <column name="instructor_hint" type="longtext"/>
        </createTable>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="exercise_id" baseTableName="metis_exercise_config" constraintName="fk_metis_exercise_config_exercise"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="exercise" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-02-create-metis-group-window" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>Create the per-tutorial-group answering window that overrides the default of the configuration.</comment>
        <createTable tableName="metis_group_window">
            <column name="id" type="bigint" autoIncrement="true">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" primaryKeyName="pk_metis_group_window" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="exercise_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="tutorial_group_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- Both required: a window that overrides the default has to say where it begins and where it ends. -->
            <column name="window_start" type="datetime(3)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="window_end" type="datetime(3)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
        <!-- One window per exercise and group. It leads with exercise_id, so it also serves that foreign key. -->
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="metis_group_window" columnNames="exercise_id, tutorial_group_id"
                             constraintName="uk_metis_group_window_exercise_group"/>
        <createIndex tableName="metis_group_window" indexName="idx_metis_group_window_tutorial_group">
            <column name="tutorial_group_id"/>
        </createIndex>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="exercise_id" baseTableName="metis_group_window" constraintName="fk_metis_group_window_config"
                                 referencedColumnNames="exercise_id" referencedTableName="metis_exercise_config" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="tutorial_group_id" baseTableName="metis_group_window" constraintName="fk_metis_group_window_tutorial_group"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="tutorial_group" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-03-create-metis-check" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>Create the per-participation check that records the state of one student's questions.</comment>
        <createTable tableName="metis_check">
            <column name="id" type="bigint" autoIncrement="true">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" primaryKeyName="pk_metis_check" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="participation_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!--
                PENDING, GENERATING, IN_REVIEW, READY, IN_PROGRESS, COMPLETED, FAILED or SKIPPED. A row is written as
                PENDING when the check is registered, before any question exists. 16 leaves room past the longest name.
            -->
            <column name="status" type="varchar(16)" defaultValue="PENDING">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- The commit the questions were generated from: a full SHA-1 of 40 hex characters, the width of build_job.commit_hash and vcs_access_log.commit_hash. -->
            <column name="commit_hash" type="varchar(40)"/>
            <!-- The model id the LLM endpoint reported, so a rating can be traced to the model that produced it. -->
            <column name="llm_model" type="varchar(100)"/>
            <!-- The version of the prompt template the questions were generated with. -->
            <column name="prompt_version" type="varchar(32)"/>
            <!-- Why the check ended FAILED or SKIPPED, e.g. no submission or an empty diff. A short phrase, not a stack trace. -->
            <column name="failure_reason" type="varchar(255)"/>
            <column name="generated_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <!-- When the first question was shown to the student. -->
            <column name="started_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <column name="completed_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
        </createTable>
        <!-- One check per participation. The unique key doubles as the supporting index of the foreign key. -->
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="metis_check" columnNames="participation_id" constraintName="uk_metis_check_participation"/>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="participation_id" baseTableName="metis_check" constraintName="fk_metis_check_participation"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="participation" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-04-create-metis-question" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>Create the question table, which holds main questions and follow-ups together with their answers and ratings.</comment>
        <createTable tableName="metis_question">
            <column name="id" type="bigint" autoIncrement="true">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" primaryKeyName="pk_metis_question" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="check_id" type="bigint">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- Display order inside the check. A follow-up takes the next free number, so main questions and follow-ups share one sequence. -->
            <column name="question_order" type="tinyint">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- Null for a main question. A follow-up names the main question it belongs to, which is in the same check. -->
            <column name="parent_question_id" type="bigint"/>
            <!--
                Main questions only: the repository-relative path of the file the question is about. A nested path can
                be longer than 255 characters with every segment valid; 1024 is what the repository file endpoints
                accept and what review_comment_thread.file_path holds.
            -->
            <column name="focus_file_path" type="varchar(1024)"/>
            <!-- Added plus deleted lines of the focus file in the diff at generation time. -->
            <column name="focus_file_changed_lines" type="int"/>
            <column name="question_text" type="longtext">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- Chosen by the LLM per question and clamped to the range in the configuration. -->
            <column name="time_limit_seconds" type="int">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- How often the question has been regenerated. -->
            <column name="regeneration_count" type="tinyint" defaultValueNumeric="0">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- When an instructor approved the question; only written when the configuration requires review. -->
            <column name="approved_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <!-- When the question was shown to the student. The time limit is measured from here on the server. -->
            <column name="shown_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <column name="answer_text" type="longtext"/>
            <column name="answered_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <!-- 1 to likert_scale_points of the configuration. -->
            <column name="llm_score" type="tinyint"/>
            <column name="llm_rationale" type="longtext"/>
            <column name="llm_rated_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
        </createTable>
        <!-- One question per position in a check. It leads with check_id, so it also serves that foreign key. -->
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="metis_question" columnNames="check_id, question_order" constraintName="uk_metis_question_check_order"/>
        <!--
            What a follow-up's key points at: a question together with its check. The id alone is unique already, so the
            pair is as well; it is declared because a foreign key can only reference a key, and the follow-up's has to
            carry the check.
        -->
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="metis_question" columnNames="id, check_id" constraintName="uk_metis_question_id_check"/>
        <!--
            Supports the follow-up key on the same pair, created before the constraint so that InnoDB adopts it. It leads
            with parent_question_id, so it also serves the lookup of a main question's follow-ups on every cascade.
        -->
        <createIndex tableName="metis_question" indexName="idx_metis_question_parent_check">
            <column name="parent_question_id"/>
            <column name="check_id"/>
        </createIndex>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="check_id" baseTableName="metis_question" constraintName="fk_metis_question_check"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="metis_check" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
        <!--
            Self-referencing, and over the pair: a follow-up names its main question together with its own check, so it
            cannot point at a question of another student's check, and deleting a main question takes its follow-ups with
            it. A main question has no parent, and a key with a null column is not checked (MATCH SIMPLE, the default of
            both databases), so main questions need nothing else. The question ids lead the pair on purpose: MySQL accepts
            the same key with check_id first, which references itself, but then enforces neither the reference nor the
            cascade.
        -->
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="parent_question_id, check_id" baseTableName="metis_question" constraintName="fk_metis_question_parent"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id, check_id" referencedTableName="metis_question" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
        The check constraints, one changeSet each: on MySQL every DDL statement commits on its own, so a changeSet that
        failed after its first statement would leave a constraint behind while staying unrecorded, and its re-run would
        stop at the duplicate name. They come before the backfill, while the configuration table is still empty, since
        MySQL rebuilds a table to add a check to it.

        MetisExerciseConfig and MetisQuestion state the same bounds for Bean Validation, but nothing that writes these
        rows runs it in production: the configuration is inserted with the column defaults by a JPQL insert, settings
        and questions change through modifying queries, and the server disables validation on persist
        (jakarta.persistence.validation.mode none). So the database holds them, each named after the column it guards.
        The column defaults satisfy all of them, and a check is evaluated on insert and update only, on the row being
        written. Liquibase's addCheckConstraint is a Pro change type, so the DDL is raw SQL, the same on every engine.

        A setting gets the range the settings offer: one to five main questions, at most three follow-ups each, time
        limits between 30 seconds and an hour, the five-step length and complexity scales, and a Likert scale of five
        or seven points and nothing in between. A follow-up is asked for a score at or below the threshold, so the
        threshold lies below the top of the scale. A later version that widens a range replaces its constraint in a
        changeSet of its own. These bounds keep every tinyint column of the table far below the 127 that MySQL's tinyint
        holds, and through the question counts the positions of a check's questions as well.

        Two of these checks tie two columns of the row together: the shortest time limit must not exceed the longest,
        and the threshold has to stay below the top of the scale. The database judges the row as each statement leaves
        it, so going from seven to five points while the threshold is 5 or 6 is refused, and so are two statements that
        write such a pair one column after the other in the wrong order. Code that changes a pair therefore validates
        the new pair first and writes both columns in one statement, as the settings update of the Metis module does.

        A question gets what holds for it whatever the settings of its exercise were when it was written: a position
        from 1, a regeneration count and a changed-line count that are not negative, a time limit inside the range any
        configuration allows, and a score on the larger of the two scales, from 1 to 7, or null until the answer is
        rated (a check passes on null). Whether a score lies on the scale of its own exercise depends on a row of
        another table, which a check constraint cannot read; the code that rates an answer has to keep it inside that
        scale. question_order and regeneration_count are tinyint: MySQL refuses a value above 127, while PostgreSQL,
        which has no tinyint and stores a smallint, would accept it. Their upper bound of 127 makes every engine refuse
        the same values, so a test on any of them finds an overflow.
    -->

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-05-check-metis-exercise-config-main-question-count" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>One to five main questions.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_main_question_count CHECK (main_question_count BETWEEN 1 AND 5);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CHECK ck_metis_exercise_config_main_question_count;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_main_question_count;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-06-check-metis-exercise-config-max-follow-ups-per-question" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>At most three follow-ups per main question; none switches them off.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_max_follow_ups_per_question CHECK (max_follow_ups_per_question BETWEEN 0 AND 3);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CHECK ck_metis_exercise_config_max_follow_ups_per_question;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_max_follow_ups_per_question;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-07-check-metis-exercise-config-seconds-per-question" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>A time limit range between 30 seconds and an hour that is not empty.</comment>
        <sql><![CDATA[ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_seconds_per_question CHECK (min_seconds_per_question >= 30 AND max_seconds_per_question <= 3600 AND min_seconds_per_question <= max_seconds_per_question);]]></sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CHECK ck_metis_exercise_config_seconds_per_question;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_seconds_per_question;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-08-check-metis-exercise-config-answer-length" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>The five-step answer length scale.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_answer_length CHECK (answer_length BETWEEN 1 AND 5);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CHECK ck_metis_exercise_config_answer_length;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_answer_length;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-09-check-metis-exercise-config-complexity" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>The five-step complexity scale.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_complexity CHECK (complexity BETWEEN 1 AND 5);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CHECK ck_metis_exercise_config_complexity;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_complexity;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-10-check-metis-exercise-config-likert-scale-points" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>A Likert scale has five or seven points; refuse any other number, six included.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_likert_scale_points CHECK (likert_scale_points IN (5, 7));</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CHECK ck_metis_exercise_config_likert_scale_points;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_likert_scale_points;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-11-check-metis-exercise-config-follow-up-threshold" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>A follow-up threshold below the top of the scale, so that the best answer never gets a follow-up.</comment>
        <sql><![CDATA[ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_follow_up_threshold CHECK (follow_up_threshold >= 1 AND follow_up_threshold < likert_scale_points);]]></sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CHECK ck_metis_exercise_config_follow_up_threshold;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_exercise_config DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_exercise_config_follow_up_threshold;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-12-check-metis-question-question-order" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>Positions start at 1 and stay inside the tinyint range on every engine.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_question ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_question_order CHECK (question_order BETWEEN 1 AND 127);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CHECK ck_metis_question_question_order;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_question_order;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-13-check-metis-question-regeneration-count" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>A regeneration count that is not negative and stays inside the tinyint range on every engine.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_question ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_regeneration_count CHECK (regeneration_count BETWEEN 0 AND 127);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CHECK ck_metis_question_regeneration_count;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_regeneration_count;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-14-check-metis-question-time-limit-seconds" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>A time limit inside the range any configuration allows.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_question ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_time_limit_seconds CHECK (time_limit_seconds BETWEEN 30 AND 3600);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CHECK ck_metis_question_time_limit_seconds;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_time_limit_seconds;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-15-check-metis-question-llm-score" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>A score on the larger of the two scales, or null until the answer is rated.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_question ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_llm_score CHECK (llm_score BETWEEN 1 AND 7);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CHECK ck_metis_question_llm_score;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_llm_score;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-16-check-metis-question-focus-file-changed-lines" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>A changed-line count that is not negative, or null for a follow-up.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE metis_question ADD CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_focus_file_changed_lines CHECK (focus_file_changed_lines >= 0);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql dbms="mysql">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CHECK ck_metis_question_focus_file_changed_lines;</sql>
            <sql dbms="postgresql,h2">ALTER TABLE metis_question DROP CONSTRAINT ck_metis_question_focus_file_changed_lines;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20261112102400-17-backfill-metis-exercise-config" author="eduardfrankford">
        <comment>Give every programming exercise that already exists its permanent Metis configuration, switched off and with the defaults.</comment>
        <!--
            New programming exercises get their row when they are stored (ExerciseConfigurationService.initialize); this
            covers the ones stored before. Only the key is written, so the column defaults supply every setting, exactly as
            for a new exercise. Exercises that already have a row are skipped, so a repeated run inserts nothing twice.
        -->
        <sql>INSERT INTO metis_exercise_config (exercise_id)
            SELECT exercise.id FROM exercise WHERE exercise.discriminator = 'P'
            AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM metis_exercise_config config WHERE config.exercise_id = exercise.id);</sql>
        <rollback>
            <comment>Only the rows that still hold every default and no group window go; a configuration an instructor changed stays.</comment>
            <sql>DELETE FROM metis_exercise_config
                WHERE enabled = FALSE AND window_start IS NULL AND window_end IS NULL AND main_question_count = 3 AND max_follow_ups_per_question = 2
                AND min_seconds_per_question = 60 AND max_seconds_per_question = 300 AND answer_length = 3 AND complexity = 3 AND likert_scale_points = 5
                AND follow_up_threshold = 3 AND review_required = FALSE AND instructor_hint IS NULL
                AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM metis_group_window group_window WHERE group_window.exercise_id = metis_exercise_config.exercise_id);</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
